  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 248 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nBreezy north winds and CAA will be the story for Friday and\nSaturday. As the occluded low slowly moves northeast into\nOntario and Quebec, high pressure from the northern plains will\nbegin to move in and tight the pressure gradient. Temperatures\nFriday and Saturday will be close to normal for this time of\nyear with high temperatures in the upper 40s Friday and low 50s\nSaturday. Areas closer to Lake Michigan could be a degree or two\nwarmer than inland areas. As we are well out of the growing\nseason there will be no headlines for frost or freeze\nThursday night through Saturday night. However, if you are among\nthe few still holding out on the garden or trying to save those\nmums for just a bit longer, Friday night will should be the \ncoldest with temperatures expected to fall into the mid 20s to\nlow 30s. This will be due to the good CAA and clearing skies \nthat are expected Friday night into Saturday. Temperatures due\nlook to trend upward again with a chance for some highs in the\nlow 60s possible early next week. From the CPC and some of the\ncluster analysis its likely that the above normal temperatures\nwill continue. Where uncertainty remains is how far above normal\nas we loose daylight and our sun angle continues to decrease. \n\nDry weather is expected to persist through the weekend and into\nearly next week. High pressure in the Northern Plains will\nslowly build east into the Great Lakes Region then linger across\nthe central CONUS through Sunday. For Monday, an upper level\ntrough and sfc low should track across Ontario and into the New\nEngland states. However, it looks like dry air is going to win\nout across southern Wisconsin. Southwest winds Monday should\nshift to northwesterly by Tuesday with the dry cold front\npassing through. Otherwise it should be largely uneventful for\nsouthern Wisconsin. \n\nPatterson",
